This is more of an enhancement than bug fix as the mentioned bug in #9133 really depends on how devloppers did implement optimistic lock, and may still have it, due to the way it was designed. A quick example is this code in `ActiveRecord::deleteInternal()` : 

```php
    $lock = $this->optimisticLock();
    if ($lock !== null) {
        $condition[$lock] = $this->$lock;
    }
    $result = static::deleteAll($condition);
    if ($lock !== null && !$result) {
        throw new StaleObjectException('The object being deleted is outdated.');
    }
```
That code assumes user have loaded a version number to model's optimistic lock attribute but it is rare to perform `$model->load()` on delete actions, either does the generated code for controllers by gii or built-in REST controllers, so the code is just getting it from database and upgrading without checking if there is a version attribute in user inputs. And there is no easy way to perform a "dirty attribute but equal to what we already have in db" check within ActiveRecord.

That said, this PR is an enhancement introducing a behavior that when implemented, ensure Optimistic Lock works properly. It parses the version value directly from `Yii::$app->request` as it is usually done with headers.
